Upstream: https://github.com/cloudflare/cloudflared/commit/3106f9499d6a23e2f87ec5267eb4771b507a4d1f
VULN-170057 (Cloudflare's own number; no CVE or GHSA yet), an Access bypass: with path
normalization on, ingress matching cleaned /public/.;x/../admin to /public/admin, so an
earlier rule without Access caught a request the origin serves as /admin, which an Access
rule protects. Matching now strips matrix (;) parameters from the escaped path first, and
an unprotected match is checked under the other reading too. In 2026.11.0 (2026-11-12),
under a week old; not in 2026.10.5. Upstream's commit as it is: its four files are
byte-identical at 2026.10.5 and at the commit's parent, and ingress_test.go is not built.
Drop this when pkg:cloudflared moves to 2026.11.0 or later.

From 3106f9499d6a23e2f87ec5267eb4771b507a4d1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Miguel da Costa Martins Marcelino
 <mdacostamartinsmarcelino@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:21:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VULN-170057: Don't bypass access with matrix parameters

Adding ingress parsing to cover matrix-path parameters. That is, paths that are delimited by semicolons. As an example, with the current normalization logic a path like /public/.;x/../admin would be normalized to /public/admin, which matches the non-Access /public rule. The new parsing logic prevents this from happening by always matching the access /admin path.

Rule matching now receives the request's URL so it can use the escaped path when normalization is enabled. URL.Path is already percent-decoded, so it cannot distinguish literal separators from encoded separators inside parameter values. For example, /admin;x=%2f..%2fpublic becomes /admin;x=/../public. Stripping parameters from that decoded path and then cleaning it incorrectly produces /public. Using URL.EscapedPath() lets us strip the parameter value first, producing /admin. When normalization is disabled, matching continues to use URL.Path.

Because path normalization now involves several steps, I refactored the code into a "pipeline" that applies transformations sequentially. This should clarify the purpose of each step.

The included tests should cover all scenarios: backslashes, trailing dots, repeated slashes, and semicolons.
